WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
This video is about Medicare and Durable&nbsp;
Medical Equipment, or DME. Let’s get started!&nbsp;

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.540
DME is equipment that helps you with your&nbsp;
activities of daily living, such as getting in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:20.760
out of bed, bathing, using the toilet, and eating.
Not every type of DME is eligible for Medicare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:27.180
coverage. Medicare usually covers DME that is&nbsp;
durable, meaning it can withstand repeated use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.540
serves a medical purpose, is appropriate for use&nbsp;
in the home, although it may also be used outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:40.320
and is likely to last for three years or more.
For example, Medicare covers wheelchairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:49.740
walkers, hospital beds, power scooters,&nbsp;
and portable oxygen equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.580
Medicare also covers prosthetics, orthotics,&nbsp;
and certain medical supplies as DME.&nbsp;

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.200
On the other hand, there are certain kinds of DME&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.560
that Medicare never covers.&nbsp;
Let’s look at some examples.&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.180
Medicare doesn’t cover equipment that’s&nbsp;
mainly used to help you outside the home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.400
For example, if you can walk on your own for&nbsp;
short distances and get around your house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.440
Medicare wouldn’t cover a motorized scooter&nbsp;
that you only need outside the home.&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.200
Second, Medicare doesn’t cover most equipment used&nbsp;
to make things more convenient or comfortable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.720
This includes stairway elevators, grab&nbsp;
bars, air conditioners, and toilet seats.&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.240
Medicare also doesn’t cover items that get&nbsp;
thrown away after being used or that aren’t used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:42.540
with equipment. For example, Medicare doesn’t&nbsp;
cover incontinence pads, surgical facemasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.340
or compression leggings, unless these items are&nbsp;
covered as part of your home health benefit.&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.840
Additionally, Medicare won’t&nbsp;
cover modifications to your home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.260
such as ramps or widened doors&nbsp;
for improving wheelchair access.&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.880
Whether you have Original Medicare or a Medicare&nbsp;
Advantage Plan, Medicare covers your DME if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.820
you meet the following two conditions. First,&nbsp;
your primary care provider must sign an order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.520
prescription, or certificate. Second, you must&nbsp;
take your prescription to the right supplier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:21.060
Be sure only to use suppliers with approval from&nbsp;
Original Medicare or your Medicare Advantage Plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.320
It’s important to note, however, that the&nbsp;
process is a little different if you need a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:31.320
manual or power wheelchair or scooter. In these&nbsp;
cases, speak with your primary care provider.&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:39.000
In most cases, Medicare Part B covers your DME.&nbsp;
Medicare normally pays eighty percent of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.100
Medicare-approved amount after you meet your Part&nbsp;
B deductible. This means you would owe a twenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:49.440
percent coinsurance for your DME if the supplier&nbsp;
charges the Medicare-approved amount for the item.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:57.240
To find a DME supplier that’s approved by&nbsp;
Medicare, visit www.medicare.gov/supplier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:03.900
Be aware that many suppliers are Medicare-approved&nbsp;
but don’t “take assignment.” If a provider doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.880
take assignment, that means they don’t accept the&nbsp;
Medicare-approved amount as the full payment for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.940
the item. These suppliers may charge more, but&nbsp;
Medicare will still only pay eighty percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:20.520
its approved amount, so you’ll be responsible for&nbsp;
the twenty percent coinsurance plus any charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.160
above the Medicare-approved amount. If you have&nbsp;
a Medicare Advantage Plan, your plan will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.140
its own cost and coverage rules for DME. You&nbsp;
should contact your plan for more information.&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:37.680
In conclusion, if you need Medicare-covered&nbsp;
DME, take the following steps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:43.080
Work with your primary care provider to get&nbsp;
a prescription for medically necessary DME.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.320
Contact Medicare or your Medicare Advantage Plan&nbsp;
to get a list of DME suppliers in your area that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.240
would provide you with the lowest costs. And&nbsp;
take your DME prescription to a supplier.&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.580
That concludes this video on Medicare and&nbsp;
Durable Medical Equipment. Thanks for watching.

